<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<activity xml:id="act-faltaInfo-introduccion" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"> 

<title>Falta de información: Introducción</title>

<statement>
  <image source="png-source/V1 3.3.20.1 Info Gap Intro Problem Card Spanish.png" width="85%">
    <shortdescription>tarjeta de problema</shortdescription>
  </image>
</statement>

<solution>
    <p>[+++++++++++++++]</p>
    <p>72 chairs. Sample response: <m>40 + (4 \times 8) = 72</m></p>
    <image source="svg-source/tikz-file-141731">
      <shortdescription>diagrama representando que 40 más cuatro veces 8 es un número desconocido</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </solution>


<!-- ============================================================== 
   Notas profesor <prelude> (se pueden mostrar previo al enunciado)                      
===================================================================  -->
<prelude component="profesor">
<p>
<dl>
 
  <!-- Estándares CCSS asociados -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="ccss-actividad-titulo"/></title> 
    <ul>
      <li> 
        <custom ref="ccss-addressing"/>        
        <xref ref="ccss-3-OA-D-8" text="custom">3.OA.D.8</xref>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </li>

  <!-- Tiempo recomendado -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="recommended-time-titulo"/></title>
    <p>15 minutos</p>
  </li>

  <!-- Introducción de la actividad -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="narrative-actividad-titulo"/></title>
    <p>[+++++++++++++++]</p>
    <p>The purpose of this activity is to introduce students to the structure of the MLR4 Information Gap routine. This routine facilitates meaningful interactions by positioning some students as holders of information that is needed by other students.</p>
    <p>Tell students that first, a demonstration will be conducted with the whole class, in which they are playing the role of the person with the problem card. Explain to students that it is the job of the person with the problem card (in this case, the whole class) to think about what information they need to answer the question.</p>
    <p>For each question that is asked, students are expected to explain what they will do with the information, by responding to the question, <q>¿Por qué necesitas saber <fillin></fillin>?</q> If the problem card person asks for information that is not on the data card (including the answer!), then the data card person must respond with, <q>No tengo esa información</q> In explaining their answers, students need to be precise in their word choice and use of language (MP6).</p>
    <p>Once the students have enough information to solve the problem, they solve the problem independently.</p>
    <p>The info gap routine requires students to make sense of problems by determining what information is necessary and then ask for information they need to solve them. This may take several rounds of discussion if their first requests do not yield the information they need (MP1).</p>
    <image source="png-source/V1 3.3.20.1 Info Gap Intro Data Card.png" width="70%">
      <shortdescription>tarjeta de datos para la introducción al gap de información</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </li>

  <!-- Launch -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="launch-titulo"/></title>
    <p>[+++++++++++++++]</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Groups of 2</li>
      <li>
        <q>En los problemas de esta lección se explora la organización de un evento especial en la escuela. ¿Cuál es su evento favorito de la escuela o de su barrio?</q>
      </li>
      <li>Share responses.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>


  <!-- Instrucciones para el desarrollo de la actividad -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="instructions-teacher-actividad-titulo"/></title>
    <p>[+++++++++++++++]</p>
    <p><alert>MLR4 Information Gap</alert></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Display the Sample Problem Card.</li>
      <li>Read the problem aloud.</li>
      <li><p>Listen for and clarify any questions about the context.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><q>Parte de la información que necesitan para resolver este problema hace falta y yo la tengo aquí. ¿Qué información específica necesitan?</q></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>1–2 minutes: quiet think time</li>
      <li>
        <q>Con su compañero, decidan qué información necesitan para resolver el problema y hagan una lista de preguntas que pueden hacer para averiguarla</q>
      </li>
      <li>2–3 minutes: partner discussion</li>
      <li>Invite students to share 1 question at a time.</li>
      <li>Record each question on a display, and respond with: 
        <q>¿Por qué necesitan saber <fillin/>?</q>  Students should provide a justification for how they will use the information before the information is revealed.
      </li>
      <li>Answer questions using only information stated on the Sample Data Card (do not reveal).</li>
      <li>Record information that is shared on the display. Give students time to decide whether they have enough information to solve the problem.</li>
      <li>Repeat until students decide they have enough information to solve, then ask students to solve.</li>
      <li>2–4 minutes: independent work time</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</dl>
</p>
</prelude>


<!-- ============================================================== 
   Notas profesor <postlude> (se pueden mostrar después del enunciado)                      
===================================================================  -->
<postlude component="profesor">
<p>
<dl>
  <!-- Síntesis de la actividad -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="synthesis-actividad-titulo"/></title>
    <p>[+++++++++++++++]</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Invite 1–2 students to share how they solved the problem.</li>
      <li>
        <q>¿Cuáles preguntas les ayudaron a encontrar cuántas sillas había en la habitación?</q>  (How many chairs were in the corner? How many chairs were in each row? How many rows were there?)
      </li>
      <li>If there were any questions that the data card cannot answer, discuss them here.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</dl>
</p>
</postlude>

</activity>